     "source": "https://slcc.pressbooks.pub/collegebiology1/chapter/incomplete-dominance-and-codominance/"
    },
    {
     "card": "RC-R1-09",
     "rule": "PATCH is a valid coat mode: distinct blocks with clear edges, each block fully one version, no blending inside a block, as in tortoiseshell and calico cats",
     "strength": "soft",
     "status": "VERIFIED",
     "source": "https://catalog.lib.kyushu-u.ac.jp/opac_download_md/7390840/7390840.pdf"
    },
    {
     "card": "RC-R1-10",
     "rule": "Each mosaic patch grows from a single cell lineage, so patches should be few and large rather than fine speckles",
     "strength": "soft",
     "status": "VERIFIED",
     "source": "https://digitalcommons.usm.maine.edu/thinking_matters/173"
    },
    {
     "card": "RC-R5-01",
     "rule": "On the chimera path, each region is fully one parent's tissue; blend between regions only at the edges, never within a patch",
     "strength": "hard",
     "status": "VERIFIED",
     "source": "https://sdbonline.org/archive/dbcinema/ledouarin/ledouarin.html"
    },
    {
     "card": "RC-R5-03",
     "rule": "Chimeras are not 50:50; allow the donor share to vary between runs, and never present a fixed even split as the biological norm",
     "strength": "soft",
     "status": "VERIFIED",
     "source": "https://www.nature.com/articles/nmeth1110-872.pdf"
    },
    {
     "card": "RC-R5-04",
     "rule": "Donor regions keep the donor's own color and surface (feathers, hair, skin), shown at full strength inside the region",
     "strength": "soft",
     "status": "VERIFIED",
     "source": "https://sdbonline.org/archive/dbcinema/ledouarin/ledouarin.html"
    },
    {
     "card": "RC-R5-05",
     "rule": "Where donor pattern appears follows the host's body layout (its skin and feather regions), not the donor's own pattern layout",
     "strength": "soft",
     "status": "VERIFIED",
     "source": "https://discovery-pp.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/10108322"
    },
    {
     "card": "RC-R5-06",
     "rule": "Donor regions align with body segments or regions (the level of a graft), not random scattered fragments",
     "strength": "soft",
     "status": "VERIFIED",
     "source": "https://sdbonline.org/archive/dbcinema/ledouarin/ledouarin.html"
    },
    {
     "card": "RC-R5-07",
     "rule": "Behavior can follow the parent that supplied the brain or head tissue",
     "strength": "soft",
     "status": "VERIFIED",
     "source": "https://agris.fao.org/search/en/records/65dfe6754c5aef494fe67490"
    },
    {
     "card": "RC-R6-02",
     "rule": "Carry the second parent mainly through surface texture, pattern and selected signature parts, which tell viewers which other animal is involved without changing what the creature is",
     "strength": "soft",
     "status": "VERIFIED",
     "source": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.12231v3"
    },
    {
     "card": "RC-R6-05",
     "rule": "Signature parts that draw looks on the real animal (a tail tuft, a trunk, an udder-like feature) are the best carriers of the second parent's identity",
     "strength": "soft",
     "status": "VERIFIED",
     "source": "https://eprints.lancs.ac.uk/id/eprint/50885/"
    },
    {
     "card": "RC-R4-09",
     "rule": "A fusion may live in a habitat neither parent uses (a bridging niche), because niche separation is what lets hybrid lineages establish; its visible traits must then match that habitat",
     "strength": "soft",
     "status": "VERIFIED",
     "source": "https://www.nature.com/articles/6886170"
    },
    {
     "card": "RC-R10-15",
     "rule": "Transfer traits, not shapes. Classify each donor feature as (a) material or covering, which grows on the host and conforms to the host's body shape; (b) homologous structure, placed where the host has the matching structure, keeping its form but fitted to host scale; (c) non-homologous structure, never transferred as a shape, only its material or function",
